" "file_sha256" "4367f77705465b81ec59d2026f225792d8e7a156e9f41f3bbc83a6718c2ef0d0"} {"question" "q-256564" "field" "field-4" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0.250"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/state.json" "file_sha256" "4367f77705465b81ec59d2026f225792d8e7a156e9f41f3bbc83a6718c2ef0d0"} {"question" "q-257204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=6{x}^{2}y+x+2y-2{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[-1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $1$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | $-4$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/state.json" "file_sha256" "4367f77705465b81ec59d2026f225792d8e7a156e9f41f3bbc83a6718c2ef0d0"} {"question" "q-257204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}y+x+2y-2{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}y+x+2y-2{y}^{2}-5{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12xy+1-10x \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+2-4y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(12xy+1-10x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(6{x}^{2}+2-4y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(12xy+1-10x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6{x}^{2}+2-4y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12y-10 & 12x \\\\ 12x & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}12y-10 & 12x \\\\ 12x & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}12xy+1-10x \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+2-4y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}12y-10 & 12x \\\\ 12x & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}12xy+1-10x \\\\ 6{x}^{2}+2-4y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}12(1)-10 & 12(-1) \\\\ 12(-1) & -4\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}12(-1)(1)+1-10(-1) \\\\ 6(-1)^{2}+2-4(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/state.json" "file_sha256" "4367f77705465b81ec59d2026f225792d8e7a156e9f41f3bbc83a6718c2ef0d0"} {"question" "q-257204" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "5cff5a87abdbca56d6fbb68b3a4c303813f45003f7abe7befaee40cea215c03a"} {"question" "q-257204" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/state.json" "file_sha256" "4367f77705465b81ec59d2026f225792d8e7a156e9f41f3bbc