lculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/example-16706.json" "file_sha256" "e66e3b0a01ac2e3ff8dccbf9ab5181560683ae6452e7a14b65a289c313a0f53b"} {"question" "e-16706" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}+3xy+1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}+3x{y}^{2}+3xy+1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4y-4 & 4x+6y+3 \\\\ 4x+6y+3 & 6x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/assets/9a89c30b5dcacbee11ea07f93458a61dd674cc8105af6f0cf5d57898b29b9787.png)\n\nStarting with\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/assets/41167d8f07938db9bfbeadb4e672e602d6dfd9c5d0b293a55952b5e8ec7a7c13.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}4y-4 & 4x+6y+3 \\\\ 4x+6y+3 & 6x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}4xy-4x+3{y}^{2}+3y \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+6xy+3x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}4(1)-4 & 4(1)+6(1)+3 \\\\ 4(1)+6(1)+3 & 6(1)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}4(1)(1)-4(1)+3(1)^{2}+3(1) \\\\ 2(1)^{2}+6(1)(1)+3(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}0 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 6-13\\cdot 13}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{169}[\\begin{aligned}-107 \\\\ -78\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.367 \\\\ 0.538\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/example-16706.json" "file_sha256" "e66e3b0a01ac2e3ff8dccbf9ab5181560683ae6452e7a14b65a289c313a0f53b"} {"question" "e-16707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2] \\times [1,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x-6y+9.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 2 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf x_1 = \\big[1, \\: 1.5 \\big]^T.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/example-16707.json" "file_sha256" "1a80cdbd0d587118673d53a60628c03c2bc4e489e67144d9d04b6701f39a71f6"} {"question" "e-16707" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3x-6y+9) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}-3x-6y+9)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-6)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/assets/ff3b9d25f1fe5ae3a7f784980aa6bce1340ea9186537f097222fbea82a596257.png)\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-6\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6(1.5)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}3(1)^{2}-3 \\\\ 3(1.5)^{2}-6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{6\\cdot 9-0\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{54}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4.5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1.417\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/example-16707.json" "file_sha256" "1a80cdbd0d587118673d53a60628c03c2bc4e489e67144d9d04b6701f39a71f6"} {"question" "e-16708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times[0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2,$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = xy^2+3x^2-xy-x.\n$$\n\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[ 1, \\: 1 \\big]^T,$ and compute the components of the first iteration" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/example-16708.json" "file_sha256" "e6899482100c741943419719f4997750de046709d2af046cefd7781f77df5ce9"} {"question" "e-16708" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f : \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n),\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nH_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+3{x}^{2}-xy-x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+6x-y-1 \\\\ 2xy-x\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+6x-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+6x-y-1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2y-1 \\\\ 2y-1 & 2x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/assets/ddb94c6d067c419b06cdbec87426e70db6ee07acca7d42c0900ae386f6fe37ae.png)\n\nStarting with ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/assets/8bdb55b26b462507d9810675499558be66e7b970d2f9fdb78e708807e617ce8d.png) we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2y-1 \\\\ 2y-1 & 2x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+6x-y-1 \\\\ 2xy-x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & 2(1)-1 \\\\ 2(1)-1 & 2(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}(1)^{2}+6(1)-(1)-1 \\\\ 2(1)(1)-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/assets/daee26a8f82011d916bf04d7e374a2b8f471602877fe76335f7f1b242ca86b00.png) Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.182 \\\\ 0.909\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14074707/example-16708.json" "file_sha256" "e6899482100c741943419719f4997750de046709d2af046cefd7781f77df5ce9"}] "usage" {"q-256562" [] "q-256567" [] "q-256564" [] "e-16706" [] "e-16708" [] "e-16707" []} "basis" 2736 "retractions" [[17592186204458 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[0, 2] \\cdot [0, 2]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = x^{2}y - 2x - y^{2} + 1$.\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $x_{0} = [1, 1]^{T}$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$[MATH: x_{1} = []$\n$1.25$\n$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186204460 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[0, 1] \\cdot [0, 1]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = 5x^{2}y - 4x + 2y^{3} - 6y^{2}$.\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1, 1]^{T}$.$[MATH: []$\n{{field-1}}\n$10$$[MATH:](x_{1} - x_{0}) = []$$-6$$[MATH:]]$$10$\n{{field-2}}\n\n{{field-3}}"] [17592186204459 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x, y)$ in the region $[0, 1] \\cdot [0, 1]⊆R^{2}$, where\n$f(x, y) = 2x^{2}y - 4xy^{2} - x + 6y^{3}$.\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1, 0]^{T}$, and compute the components of the first iteration, rounding your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).$[MATH: []$$0$\n{{field-1}}\n$[MATH:](x_{1} - x_{0}) = []$\n{{field-2}}\n$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-3}}\n$-8$$-2$\n$[MATH: x_{1} = []$$1$$[MATH:]]$\n{{field-4}}"] [17592186203777 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1] \\times [0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$f(x,y) = 2x^2y-2x^2+3xy^2+3xy+1$.\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed)."] [17592186203777 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x^{2}y - 2x^{2} + 3xy^{2} + 3xy + 1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x^{2}y - 2x^{2} + 3xy^{2} + 3xy + 1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4xy - 4x + 3y^{2} + 3y \\\\ 2x^{2} + 6xy + 3x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy - 4x + 3y^{2} + 3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x^{2} + 6xy + 3x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy - 4x + 3y^{2} + 3y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x^{2} + 6xy + 3x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4y - 4 & 4x + 6y + 3 \\\\ 4x + 6y + 3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4y - 4 & 4x + 6y + 3 \\\\ 4x + 6y + 3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} - \\begin{bmatrix}4y - 4 & 4x + 6y + 3 \\\\ 4x + 6y + 3 & 6x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4xy - 4x + 3y^{2} + 3y \\\\ 2x^{2} + 6xy + 3x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}4(1) - 4 & 4(1) + 6(1) + 3 \\\\ 4(1) + 6(1) + 3 & 6(1)\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}4(1)(1) - 4(1) + 3(1)^{2} + 3(1) \\\\ 2(1)^{2} + 6(1)(1) + 3(1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 13 \\\\ 13 & 6\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{0 \\cdot 6 - 13 \\cdot 13}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -13 \\\\ -13 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\frac{1}{169}\\begin{bmatrix}-107 \\\\ -78\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}0.367 \\\\ 0.538\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186203778 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[1,2] \\times [1,2] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$f(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x-6y+9$.\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at $\\mathbf x_0 = \\big[1, \\: 2 \\big]^T$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives $\\mathbf x_1 = \\big[1, \\: 1.5 \\big]^T$. Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed)."] [17592186203778 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{3} + y^{3} - 3x - 6y + 9) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{3} + y^{3} - 3x - 6y + 9)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 3 \\\\ 3y^{2} - 6\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x^{2} - 3) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y^{2} - 6) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x^{2} - 3) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y^{2} - 6)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{2} &= x_{1} - \\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6y\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{1}}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 3 \\\\ 3y^{2} - 6\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{1}} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6(1) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6(1.5)\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}3(1)^{2} - 3 \\\\ 3(1.5)^{2} - 6\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{bmatrix}^{-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{6 \\cdot 9 - 0 \\cdot 0}\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0.75\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.5\\end{bmatrix} - \\frac{1}{54}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 4.5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1.417\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186203779 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times[0,1] \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^2$, where\n$f(x,y) = xy^2+3x^2-xy-x$.\nFind the matrix equation that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $\\big[1, \\: 1 \\big]^T$, and compute the components of the first iteration"] [17592186203779 :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f: \\mathbb{R}^n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} = \\mathbf{x}_n - [H_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)]^{-1} \\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nwhere $H(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ and $\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at $\\mathbf{x}_n$.\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nH_f(\\mathbf{x}_n)(\\mathbf{x}_{n+1} - \\mathbf{x}_n) = -\\nabla{f}(\\mathbf{x}_n)\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∇f(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy^{2} + 3x^{2} - xy - x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy^{2} + 3x^{2} - xy - x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}y^{2} + 6x - y - 1 \\\\ 2xy - x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nH_{f}(x) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y^{2} + 6x - y - 1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy - x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y^{2} + 6x - y - 1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy - x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y - 1 \\\\ 2y - 1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y - 1 \\\\ 2y - 1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nStarting with $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2y - 1 \\\\ 2y - 1 & 2x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= -\\begin{bmatrix}y^{2} + 6x - y - 1 \\\\ 2xy - x\\end{bmatrix}_{x_{0}} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2(1) - 1 \\\\ 2(1) - 1 & 2(1)\\end{bmatrix}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= -\\begin{bmatrix}(1)^{2} + 6(1) - (1) - 1 \\\\ 2(1)(1) - 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}(x_{1} - x_{0}) &= \\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $\\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix}$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= x_{0} + \\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}-9/11 \\\\ -1/11\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&\\approx \\begin{bmatrix}0.182 \\\\ 0.909\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [[17592186202988 :answer-field/id] [17592186202988 :answer-field/key] [17592186202988 :answer-field/type] [17592186202989 :answer-field/id] [17592186202989 :answer-field/key] [17592186202989 :answer-field/type] [17592186202990 :answer-field/id] [17592186202990 :answer-field/key] [17592186202990 :answer-field/type] [17592186202991 :answer-field/id] [17592186202991 :answer-field/key] [17592186202991 :answer-field/type] [17592186202992 :answer-field/id] [17592186202992 :answer-field/key] [17592186202992 :answer-field/type] [17592186202993 :answer-field/id] [17592186202993 :answer-field/key] [17592186202993 :answer-field/type] [17592186202994 :answer-field/id] [17592186202994 :answer-field/key] [17592186202994 :answer-field/type] [17592186202995 :answer-field/id] [17592186202995 :answer-field/key] [17592186202995 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
